   "source": [
    "# The 3 largest readings for each meter, with the exact timestamp, so we can judge if they're real or glitches.\n",
    "top = (raw[POWER_COLS].stack().rename('kW').reset_index()     # stack(): wide table -> long list of (Date, meter, value)\n",
    "       .rename(columns={'level_1': 'meter'})\n",
    "       .sort_values('kW', ascending=False)                    # biggest first\n",
    "       .groupby('meter').head(3)                              # keep the top 3 per meter\n",
    "       .sort_values(['meter', 'kW'], ascending=[True, False]))\n",
    "top"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "a990c16d",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Some maxima are physically implausible **single-minute glitches**, e.g. `z3_Light` = 4,762 kW (median ≈ 20 kW),\n",
    "`z2_AC2` = 798 kW (normal max ≈ 80), `z4_Light` = 512 kW, `z2_AC3` = 25 kW on a meter that otherwise reads 0.01.\n",
    "\n",
    "Rule used to flag spikes: a reading is a spike if it is **more than 3× the meter's 99.9th percentile _and_ the excursion\n",
    "lasts ≤ 3 minutes _and_ the surrounding 31-minute median is normal (< cap/3)**. The duration condition matters: `z3_Plug` has sustained multi-minute runs at 15–20 kW (a real device\n",
    "switching on), which a pure threshold would wrongly delete."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
